; NEGATIVE ZERO, which JavaScript produces and the other three do not.
; inputs: 1 2 6
; expect: 0 0 3
;
; `Math.trunc(-1 / 2)` is `-0` on V8, and so is `-2 % 2`, wherever the true
; result is zero and the dividend is negative. Go, Java and x86 all give `0`.
;
; IT HIDES ALMOST EVERYWHERE, which is why this case prints the value RAW.
; `-0 === 0` is true, `String(-0)` is `"0"`, and every arithmetic operation
; normalises it -- `-0 + 5` is `5` -- so a case that combined the quotient into
; a larger number would pass while the bug was present. `console.log` is the one
; thing that shows it, printing `-0`.
;
; So the wrong answer here is not a different NUMBER, it is a different SIGN on
; zero, and only an unmodified value carries it.
;
; The third row is the control: an ordinary division that is not zero at all, so
; the case cannot pass by returning 0 for everything.
;
; The dividend comes out of a buffer because reduction does not execute a
; `build` -- with a literal the whole division would fold at compile time and
; never reach a backend.
(def run (fn (n)
  (let (build 2 (fn (b) (set (set b 0 (- 0 n)) 1 n))) (fn (t)
    (if (= n 1) (/ (t 0) 2)
      (if (= n 2) (% (t 0) 2)
        (/ (t 1) 2)))))))
